'''acuminate''' (a̤-kū′mi-nāt), ''v.''; pret. and pp. ''acuminated'', ppr. ''acuminating''. [< L. ''acuminatus'', pp. of ''acuminare'', sharpen, < ''acumen'', a point: see ''acumen''.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' To bring to a point; render sharp or keen: as, “to ''acuminate'' despair,” ''Cowper'', Letters, p. 172. [Rare, except in the past participle.]
{{smaller block|This is not ''acuminated'' and pointed, as in the rest, but seemeth, as it were, cut off.
{{right|''Sir T. Browne'', Vulg. Err.}}}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To taper or rise to a point. [Obsolete, except in the present participle.]
{{smaller block|They [the bishops], . . . ''acuminating'' still higher and higher in a cone of prelaty, instead of healing up the gashes of the church, . . . fall to gore one another with their sharp spires, for upper places and precedence.
{{right|''Milton'', Church Gov., i.}}}}

'''acuminate''' (a̤-kū′mi-nạ̄t), ''a.'' [< L. ''acuminatus'', pp.: see the verb.] Pointed; acute. {{smaller|Specifically —(''a'') In ''bot.'', having a long, tapering}}
